---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Projects"
  ><section class="page-hero shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Projects</p><h1>Systems worth<br />building carefully.</h1><p class="lede">
      Selected work distilled from daily notes and project logs. Employer/customer-specific details
      are sanitized; the focus is on architecture, tradeoffs, and engineering judgment.
    </p>
  </section><section class="shell projects">
    <article class="project featured">
      <p class="eyebrow">Featured · Platform observability</p><h2>Multi-tenant Grafana RBAC</h2><p
        class="project-subtitle"
      >
        Keycloak-driven org mapping with Terraform-managed Grafana state
      </p><p>
        Designed a multi-tenant observability model using Grafana Organizations, Keycloak group
        claims, and Terraform-managed orgs, datasources, dashboards, folders, alert rules, contact
        points, and notification policies.
      </p><p>
        The core decision: keep the bundle responsible for Grafana as an application while Terraform
        owns Grafana content and tenant state. That avoids cross-environment org ID skew, enables
        drift detection, and makes new team onboarding a config-driven change.
      </p><div class="tags">
        Grafana · Keycloak · Terraform · Atmos · Loki · Prometheus · CloudWatch
      </div><p class="muted">
        Sanitized from project notes covering ADR work, local k3d validation, UDS Core bundle
        configuration, and Grafana dashboard onboarding workflows.
      </p>
    </article><article class="project">
      <p class="eyebrow">Infrastructure as code</p><h2>Platform modernization patterns</h2><p>
        Refactored deployment and runner infrastructure toward consistent Atmos/Terraform stack
        patterns: remote state, promotion workflows, policy validation, JSON schema validation, and
        approval gates.
      </p><div class="tags">AWS · EKS · Terraform · Atmos · GitLab CI/CD · CodePipeline</div><span
        class="status">In practice</span
      >
    </article><article class="project">
      <p class="eyebrow">Operational reliability</p><h2>Database restore optimization</h2><p>
        Compared PostgreSQL restore approaches and documented the faster operational path: avoid
        fragile S3 streaming, use mounted object storage, parallel <code>pg_restore</code>, and choose
        the instance path that wins end-to-end time rather than benchmark time alone.
      </p><div class="tags">PostgreSQL · RDS · S3 · Runbooks · Performance testing</div><span
        class="status">Documented runbook</span
      >
    </article><article class="project">
      <p class="eyebrow">Product build · In design</p><h2>Heads-up</h2><p>
        A planned B2B crawfish marketplace designed around reliable transaction boundaries: Next.js
        web app, Go Connect-RPC API, Postgres state machines, Keycloak auth, Stripe Connect, and an
        outbox worker so state changes and side effects stay atomic.
      </p><div class="tags">Next.js · Go · Postgres · Keycloak · Stripe · Outbox pattern</div><span
        class="status">Private build notes</span
      >
    </article><article class="project">
      <p class="eyebrow">Future public data project</p><h2>12th Data</h2><p>
        An open-source data platform concept combining data engineering, analytics, infrastructure,
        and visualization around public Texas A&amp;M football data.
      </p><div class="tags">Python · SQL · PostgreSQL · Parquet · Docker · Terraform</div><span
        class="status">Planned</span
      >
    </article>
  </section></BaseLayout
>
